WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.266
Roll two four-sided dice.

2
00:00:05.266 --> 00:00:09.051
Each one shows one, two, three or four.

3
00:00:09.051 --> 00:00:13.215
That gives sixteen equally likely outcomes

4
00:00:13.215 --> 00:00:15.959
the first die picks the row

5
00:00:15.959 --> 00:00:19.460
and the second die picks the column.

6
00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.304
At a school fair

7
00:00:21.304 --> 00:00:24.731
you win the larger of the two numbers

8
00:00:24.731 --> 00:00:25.610
in coins.

9
00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:28.510
Here is the prize in every cell.

10
00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:30.970
A ticket costs three coins.

11
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.534
Is the average prize more than three coins?

12
00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:37.200
Pause and guess.

13
00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.531
Take one outcome: the first die shows one

14
00:00:44.531 --> 00:00:46.585
the second shows four.

15
00:00:46.585 --> 00:00:48.282
The prize is four.

16
00:00:48.372 --> 00:00:51.319
So the prize is a rule that turns

17
00:00:51.408 --> 00:00:53.820
each outcome into a number.

18
00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.477
That is exactly what a random variable is

19
00:00:57.477 --> 00:01:00.547
a function, X, from the sample space S

20
00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:02.279
to a set of values V.

21
00:01:02.279 --> 00:01:05.822
Nothing about the function itself is random.

22
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.869
The randomness is only in

23
00:01:07.947 --> 00:01:10.467
which outcome happens, this one

24
00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:11.490
or this one.

25
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.110
The same outcome can feed other functions.

26
00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:18.147
The sum of the two numbers gives five.

27
00:01:18.147 --> 00:01:20.561
And the values can be any set

28
00:01:20.561 --> 00:01:23.520
win or lose is a random variable too.

29
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.199
Each value of M picks out an event

30
00:01:27.199 --> 00:01:30.652
the outcomes where M takes that value.

31
00:01:30.652 --> 00:01:33.397
M equals one is a single cell.

32
00:01:33.397 --> 00:01:35.965
M equals two is three cells.

33
00:01:35.965 --> 00:01:38.621
M equals three is five cells

34
00:01:38.621 --> 00:01:41.100
and M equals four is seven.

35
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:44.519
Each event is an L-shaped layer

36
00:01:44.519 --> 00:01:48.354
and the sizes are the odd numbers: one

37
00:01:48.354 --> 00:01:50.272
three, five, seven.

38
00:01:50.368 --> 00:01:53.437
So the probability that M equals

39
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:55.930
four is seven sixteenths.

40
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.435
Stack the counts as columns.

41
00:01:58.435 --> 00:02:00.976
Each value with its probability

42
00:02:00.976 --> 00:02:03.747
that list is the distribution of M.

43
00:02:03.747 --> 00:02:06.749
The columns hold sixteen cells in all

44
00:02:06.749 --> 00:02:09.520
so the probabilities add up to one.

45
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.570
Here is another random variable

46
00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:14.961
on the same dice: the sum, T.

47
00:02:14.961 --> 00:02:16.714
The grid is the same

48
00:02:16.714 --> 00:02:20.300
but every cell now holds a different number.

49
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:24.600
The distribution of the sum is a tent: one

50
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.815
two, three, four, three, two, one.

51
00:02:27.815 --> 00:02:30.755
Because the values are numbers

52
00:02:30.755 --> 00:02:33.878
T less than five is an event too.

53
00:02:33.878 --> 00:02:35.531
It has six cells

54
00:02:35.531 --> 00:02:38.930
so its probability is three eighths.

55
00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:43.152
When are two random variables independent?

56
00:02:43.152 --> 00:02:46.880
When, for every value a and every value b

57
00:02:46.967 --> 00:02:50.782
the probability that X equals a and Y equals

58
00:02:50.868 --> 00:02:54.510
b is the product of the two probabilities.

59
00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.475
Take X, the first die, and Y, the second.

60
00:02:58.475 --> 00:03:01.562
X equals two is a row: four cells

61
00:03:01.562 --> 00:03:02.708
one quarter.

62
00:03:02.708 --> 00:03:05.177
Y equals three is a column

63
00:03:05.177 --> 00:03:06.765
also one quarter.

64
00:03:06.765 --> 00:03:10.468
Both together is one cell, one sixteenth

65
00:03:10.468 --> 00:03:14.260
and that is one quarter times one quarter.

66
00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.600
The same happens for every

67
00:03:16.677 --> 00:03:18.284
row and every column

68
00:03:18.284 --> 00:03:20.810
so the two dice are independent.

69
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:25.309
Now take X, the first die, and M, the prize.

70
00:03:25.309 --> 00:03:28.249
X equals four is the bottom row.

71
00:03:28.338 --> 00:03:30.654
M equals four is the seven

72
00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:32.969
cells of the outer layer.

73
00:03:32.969 --> 00:03:36.265
Both together: the whole bottom row

74
00:03:36.265 --> 00:03:38.670
four cells out of sixteen.

75
00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.873
But the product is one quarter

76
00:03:41.968 --> 00:03:44.156
times seven sixteenths

77
00:03:44.156 --> 00:03:47.009
which is seven sixty-fourths.

78
00:03:47.009 --> 00:03:48.055
Not equal.

79
00:03:48.055 --> 00:03:51.003
Once the first die shows four

80
00:03:51.003 --> 00:03:54.142
the prize is certain to be four.

81
00:03:54.142 --> 00:03:56.900
X and M are not independent.

82
00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:58.418
A warning.

83
00:03:58.418 --> 00:04:00.481
Take X and the sum T

84
00:04:00.481 --> 00:04:03.762
at a equals one and b equals five.

85
00:04:03.762 --> 00:04:05.357
That is row one

86
00:04:05.357 --> 00:04:08.545
and the four cells with sum five.

87
00:04:08.545 --> 00:04:11.920
They share one cell: one sixteenth.

88
00:04:12.014 --> 00:04:15.483
And one quarter times four sixteenths

89
00:04:15.577 --> 00:04:17.640
is also one sixteenth.

90
00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.284
So are they independent?

91
00:04:20.284 --> 00:04:22.770
Try b equals two instead.

92
00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:26.307
Sum two is one cell, inside row one

93
00:04:26.307 --> 00:04:27.741
one sixteenth.

94
00:04:27.836 --> 00:04:30.704
But the product is one quarter

95
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.910
times one sixteenth, only one sixty-fourth.

96
00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:38.358
One pair that works proves nothing.

97
00:04:38.358 --> 00:04:42.340
Independence is a statement about all pairs.

98
00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:44.788
So here is the pattern.

99
00:04:44.869 --> 00:04:47.630
To prove that two random variables

100
00:04:47.711 --> 00:04:51.284
are independent, check every pair of values.

101
00:04:51.284 --> 00:04:54.614
To refute it, one pair where the product

102
00:04:54.695 --> 00:04:56.400
rule fails is enough.

103
00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:59.976
The first die and the prize: refuted

104
00:04:59.976 --> 00:05:01.546
at four and four.

105
00:05:01.546 --> 00:05:05.470
The two dice: proved, for all sixteen pairs.

106
00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:07.157
Try it yourself

107
00:05:07.226 --> 00:05:09.510
are the larger and the smaller of

108
00:05:09.579 --> 00:05:11.517
the two numbers independent?

109
00:05:11.517 --> 00:05:12.970
One pair settles it.

110
00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.049
In part two we come back to the fair.

111
00:05:16.049 --> 00:05:19.368
The average prize is called the expectation

112
00:05:19.441 --> 00:05:21.138
and we will see whether

113
00:05:21.211 --> 00:05:22.760
it beats three coins.
